{m:4,d:-1,t:`Course summary and exam prep`,s:`Seven skills, one approach, and how to pass`,
goal:`By the end, you can connect all seven skills into one way of working, handle scenario questions with a clear method, and you know how to prepare for the CCAO-F exam.`,
body:`<div class="card"><h3>The 7 skills as one approach</h3><ul><li><b>Set up:</b> choose entry point, features, model and context.</li><li><b>Ask:</b> structured prompts (RCGCF), tags, examples, steps.</li><li><b>Check:</b> accuracy, completeness, sources, logic, tone, bias, review by risk.</li><li><b>Design:</b> classify tasks, chain steps, standardise, measure, set expectations.</li><li><b>Configure:</b> Projects, clean knowledge, connectors, owners.</li><li><b>Govern:</b> classify data, mask, follow policy, watch for injection.</li><li><b>Fix:</b> find the root cause, change one thing, test.</li></ul><p style="margin:8px 0 0">Behind all seven sit the <b>4Ds</b> of AI Fluency: Delegation, Description, Discernment, Diligence (Lesson 8).</p></div>

<h2>One scenario, all seven skills</h2>
<div class="card"><p><b>Situation:</b> Meera leads a 6-person customer success team. Every Monday they write account updates for 40 clients. It takes the team a full day.</p>
<ol><li><b>Set up:</b> the team uses the company-approved Claude workspace. A balanced model is enough; nothing needs web search.</li>
<li><b>Configure:</b> a Project "Client updates" with instructions (audience, tone, 150 words, format) and the current account template. Owner: Meera.</li>
<li><b>Govern:</b> they include only the data needed: no personal phone numbers, no contract values not needed in the update.</li>
<li><b>Ask:</b> one standard RCGCF prompt with tags for the notes and 2 example updates.</li>
<li><b>Design:</b> chain = extract actions → draft update → check against checklist → account manager approves (Augment, with a human check before sending).</li>
<li><b>Check:</b> account managers verify dates, numbers and promises in each update.</li>
<li><b>Fix:</b> week 2 updates were too long; they added "under 150 words" and an example, tested on 5 clients, and saved v2.</li></ol>
<p><b>Result:</b> the work drops from a day to a morning, quality is consistent, and every update is still checked by a person.</p></div>

<h2>Which exam guide applies to you?</h2>
<div class="say"><b>Important: the exam changes on 7 December 2026</b>Your <b>appointment date</b> decides the version. Before 7 December 2026: exam guide <b>v1.0</b> (7 domains). On or after 7 December 2026: exam guide <b>v1.1</b> (6 domains). This course covers both. Always read the official guide on Anthropic's site before you book.</div>
<div class="grid2"><div class="card"><h3>Guide v1.0 (before 7 Dec 2026)</h3><div class="scroll"><table><tbody>
<tr><td>1. Prompting &amp; Task Execution</td><td>14%</td></tr><tr><td>2. Output Evaluation &amp; Validation</td><td>21%</td></tr><tr><td>3. Product &amp; Model Selection</td><td>12%</td></tr><tr><td>4. Workflow Integration &amp; Solution Design</td><td>16%</td></tr><tr><td>5. Configuration &amp; Knowledge Management</td><td>12%</td></tr><tr><td>6. Governance, Risk &amp; Responsible Use</td><td>15%</td></tr><tr><td>7. Troubleshooting &amp; Optimization</td><td>10%</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Guide v1.1 (from 7 Dec 2026)</h3><div class="scroll"><table><tbody>
<tr><td>1. Task Definition and Prompting</td><td>15%</td></tr><tr><td>2. Output Evaluation and Quality Control</td><td>20%</td></tr><tr><td>3. Capability, Model, and Context Selection</td><td>15%</td></tr><tr><td>4. Workflow Design and Integration</td><td>18%</td></tr><tr><td>5. Configuration and Knowledge Management</td><td>15%</td></tr><tr><td>6. Responsible Use, Data Protection, and Governance</td><td>17%</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div>
<p>See the <a href="#blueprint">Exam blueprint</a> page for every objective, mapped to the lessons here, with a self-check box for each.</p>

<h2>What's new in v1.1 (in one list)</h2>
<ul><li>Troubleshooting is no longer its own domain; it is spread across prompting, configuration and workflow.</li>
<li>New and stronger topics: <b>code-executed analysis</b>, <b>Skills</b>, <b>memory and incognito chats</b>, <b>effort level</b>, delegation labels (<b>AI-executed / human-reviewed / human-retained</b>) and <b>over-delegation</b>, output verdicts (<b>ready / needs revision / needs human correction</b>), use-case verdicts (<b>appropriate / with safeguards / inappropriate</b>), <b>third-party Skill and connector trust</b>, the <b>Anthropic Usage Policy</b>, <b>policy gap vs training gap</b>, and finding the <b>broken layer</b> of a setup.</li>
<li>The four <b>AI Fluency competencies</b> (Delegation, Description, Discernment, Diligence) are in scope. See Lesson 8.</li>
<li>Out of scope: remembering prices, context-window sizes and rate limits.</li></ul>

<h2>Exam facts</h2>
<div class="scroll"><table class="stack"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Guide v1.0</th><th>Guide v1.1</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td data-l="Item">Questions</td><td data-l="v1.0">60</td><td data-l="v1.1">70 in total: 60 scored + 10 unscored (you can't tell which)</td></tr>
<tr><td data-l="Item">Types</td><td data-l="v1.0" colspan="2">Scenario-based multiple choice and multiple response; each question says how many answers to pick</td></tr>
<tr><td data-l="Item">Time</td><td data-l="v1.0" colspan="2">120 minutes</td></tr>
<tr><td data-l="Item">Pass mark</td><td data-l="v1.0" colspan="2">Scaled score of 720 (scale 100–1,000). Fixed standard, not compared with other people</td></tr>
<tr><td data-l="Item">Score report</td><td data-l="v1.0" colspan="2">Pass/fail, scaled score and % correct per domain (domain % is for information only)</td></tr>
<tr><td data-l="Item">Price</td><td data-l="v1.0" colspan="2">$99 USD per attempt</td></tr>
<tr><td data-l="Item">Delivery</td><td data-l="v1.0" colspan="2">Proctored online or at a Pearson VUE test centre; register through the Anthropic Partner Academy</td></tr>
<tr><td data-l="Item">Prerequisites</td><td data-l="v1.0" colspan="2">None. Hands-on use of Claude at work is recommended</td></tr>
<tr><td data-l="Item">Valid for</td><td data-l="v1.0" colspan="2">12 months from the award date</td></tr></tbody></table></div>

<h2>Rules you should know before exam day</h2>
<div class="grid2"><div class="card"><h3>ID and booking</h3><ul><li>Bring a valid, unexpired, government-issued photo ID. The name must match your registration exactly.</li><li>Need accommodations? Get them approved by Pearson before you schedule.</li><li>Change or cancel: online exams up to 1 hour before, test centre up to 48 hours before. Later changes lose the fee.</li><li>No-show or very late: the fee is lost and you must register again.</li></ul></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Retakes, renewal, appeals</h3><ul><li>Waiting time after a fail: 14 days (1st), 30 days (2nd), 90 days (3rd).</li><li>Up to 4 attempts in a rolling 12 months; pay for each attempt.</li><li>Renew on time with a free, non-proctored assessment on the Partner Academy. A lapsed credential needs a full retake.</li><li>Appeals within 14 days; individual question content can't be appealed.</li></ul></div></div>

<h2>The style of the official sample questions</h2>
<p>The official guides include sample questions. Their lessons, in our own words:</p>
<div class="grid3"><div class="card"><h3>Output checks</h3><p>A confident summary cites a precise subsection, statistic or quote. <b>Right move:</b> verify each one against the original source; remove or flag what you can't confirm. Claude's own "I'm sure" is not verification.</p></div><div class="card"><h3>Model choice</h3><p>Many short, simple drafts needed fast and cheap. <b>Right move:</b> a faster, lower-cost model. Save the top model for complex reasoning.</p></div><div class="card"><h3>Data protection</h3><p>A sheet has names and account numbers; policy limits personal data. <b>Right move:</b> remove or anonymise identifiers before upload, as policy says. Don't abandon the task; don't upload first.</p></div></div>

<h2>How to answer scenario questions</h2>
<ol><li><b>Read the last line first:</b> what exactly is being asked? "Best", "first", "most likely cause"?</li>
<li><b>Find the real problem</b> in the scenario: is it missing context? risky data? a stale file? wrong model?</li>
<li><b>Name the domain:</b> this tells you which lesson's rules apply.</li>
<li><b>Remove the clearly wrong answers:</b> "never use AI", "trust it fully", "skip review", "paste everything".</li>
<li><b>Choose the safest, most practical</b> answer that fixes the root cause.</li></ol>
<div class="grid2"><div class="card bad"><h3>Answers that are usually wrong</h3><ul><li>"Always use the biggest model"</li><li>"No review needed"</li><li>"Upload everything just in case"</li><li>"Stop using AI completely"</li><li>"Just retry"</li></ul></div>
<div class="card good"><h3>Answers that are usually right</h3><ul><li>Match the model to the task</li><li>Review in proportion to risk</li><li>One current source of truth</li><li>Mask data and follow policy</li><li>Find the root cause, change one thing</li></ul></div></div>
<p><b>Multiple response questions:</b> the question tells you how many to pick (for example "Select TWO"). Judge each option on its own as true or false, then pick exactly that number.</p>

<h2>Study plan</h2>
<div class="grid2"><div class="card"><h3>Study plan</h3><ul><li>Spend the most time on Domain 2 (21%) and Domain 4 (16%).</li><li>Practise in Claude every day, on real work tasks.</li><li>Do this site's practice exam, then review your weakest domain.</li><li>Take the official prep course on Anthropic Academy.</li><li>Teach one lesson to a friend: teaching shows your gaps.</li></ul></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Exam-day tips</h3><ul><li>Read the scenario first: what is the real problem?</li><li>Look for the <b>safest, most practical</b> answer, not the most technical.</li><li>Watch for "select TWO" questions.</li><li>Flag hard questions and come back.</li><li>About 2 minutes per question: don't get stuck.</li></ul></div></div>

<h2>Final checklist before you book</h2>
<div class="card"><ul><li>I can explain the 4 setup choices and pick a model for a scenario.</li><li>I can write an RCGCF prompt with tags and an example.</li><li>I can list the 8 output checks and match review to risk.</li><li>I can classify tasks and design a prompt chain with a human check.</li><li>I can set up a clean Project and explain when to use a connector.</li><li>I can classify and mask data and spot prompt injection.</li><li>I can use the 5-step fix method.</li><li>I scored above 72% on the practice exam in every domain.</li></ul></div>`,
say:`Set up, ask, check, design, configure, govern, fix. That's the Associate way of working.`,
act:`Take the practice exam on this site. Then spend 30 minutes on your weakest domain, and tick the final checklist.`,
